{
  "result": "There's a particular kind of light that arrives at five in the morning, blue and tentative, as if the day is still deciding whether to begin. I like to be awake for it, coffee in hand, watching the world reassemble itself from shadows. Nothing is urgent yet. Everything feels possible today.\n\nThe kettle hums, the dog sighs in her sleep, and the news has not started shouting. My notebook lies open, half filled with yesterday's unfinished thoughts. I add a new line without judgment. Morning forgiveness is real. You get to start over quietly before anyone expects you to be brilliant.\n\nI often think creativity is misunderstood as lightning when it is plumbing. It is showing up, tightening the valves, clearing the clogs of doubt. Ideas do not strike the perfect. They leak toward the persistent. Write badly, walk slowly, notice everything. The marvelous hides inside the mundane, waiting for attention.\n\nYesterday I watched an old man feed pigeons with seriousness, as if appointing ambassadors. A child asked why the sky is blue, and her mother said because it likes us. Both answers felt true enough. We live less by facts than by stories that make the day bearable and bright.\n\nSo this is my free two hundred fifty words: no argument, no moral, just a morning, a metaphor, some pigeons. If you are reading this with your own coffee going cold, consider this permission to wander. Pay attention to light, be kind to beginners including yourself, and begin again tomorrow.",
